          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            a limit of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to C
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim F
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            equipped with morphisms to the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(d)
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim F
            is the
            %em universal
            object with this property, i.e. the “most optimized solution” to the problem of finding such an object.
          %p
            The limit construction has a wealth of applications throughout category theory and mathematics in general. In practice, it is possibly best thought of in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functors
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            for maps into a diagram. So in some sense the limit object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim F
            “subsumes” the entire diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(D)
            into a single object, as far as morphisms
            %em into
            it are concerned. The corresponding universal object for morphisms
            %em out
            of the diagram is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
          %p
            An intuitive general idea is that a limit of a diagram is the locus or solution set of a bunch of equations, where each of the coordinates is parametrized by one of the objects of the diagram, and where the equations are prescribed by the morphisms of the diagram. This idea is explained more formally
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/limits+and+colimits+by+example#limitsintermsofotherops"} here
          %p
            Often, the general theory of limits (but not colimits!) works better if the source of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is taken to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^op
            (or equivalently, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is taken to be a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functor"} contravariant functor
          %p In some cases the category-theoretic notion of limit does reproduce notions of limit as known from analysis. See the examples below.
          %h3#global_versus_local Global versus local
          %p
            In correspondence to the
            %em local
            definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functors
            (as discussed there), there is a
            %em local
            definition of limits (in terms of cones), that defines a limit (if it exists) for each individual diagram, and there is a
            %em global
            definition, which defines the limit for
            %em all
            diagrams (in terms of an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint
          %p If all limits over the given shape of diagrams exist in a category, then both definitions are equivalent.
          %p
            See also the analogous discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
          %h2#terminology_and_notation Terminology and notation
          %p
            A limit is taken over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D^{op} \to C
            and since the functor comes equipped with the information about what its domain is, one can just write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim F
            for its limit. But often it is helpful to indicate how the functor is evaluated on objects, in which case the limit is written
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi D
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_{d \in D} F(d)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is given by a formula (as with other notation with bound variables.)
          %p
            In some schools of mathematics, limits are called
            = succeed "," do
              %em projective limits
            = succeed "." do
              %em inductive limits
            %em inverse limits
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em direct limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-object"} ind-object
          %p
            Correspondingly, the symbols
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo ←
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{\leftarrow}lim
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{\rightarrow}\lim
            are used instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} colim
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \colim
          %p
            Confusingly, many authors restrict the meanings of these alternative terms to (co)limits whose sources are
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction"} directed sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+limit"} directed limit
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#local_definition_in_terms_of_representable_functors Local definition in terms of representable functors
          %p There is a general abstract definition of limits in terms of representable functors, which we describe now. This reproduces the more concrete and maybe more familiar description in terms of universal cones, which is described further below.
          %p
            Let in the following
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            the category of sets (possibly realized as the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U Set
            of
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
          %h4#limit_of_a_setvalued_functor Limit of a Set-valued functor
          %p
            The
            %strong limit of a Set-valued functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D^{op} \to Set
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  lim F \coloneqq Hom_{[D^{op}, Set]}(pt, F)
                  \\in Set
          %p
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D^{op}, Set]
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            category), where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  pt : D^{op} \to Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mo :
                  %mi d
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  pt : d \mapsto \{*\}
          %p
            is the functor constant on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            diagram.
          %p
            The set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim F
            is equivalently called
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the set of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global sections
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %li
              %p
                the set of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} global elements
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            The set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim F
            can be equivalently expressed as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi D
                    %mo ∈
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi D
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ∀
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi i
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mi i
                    %mo }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  lim F \simeq
                  \\left\lbrace
                  (x_d)_{d \in D}
                  \\in
                  \\prod_{d \in D}
                  F(d)
                  |
                  \\forall (d_i \stackrel{\alpha}{\to} d_j) \in D :
                  F(\alpha)(x_{d_j}) = x_{d_i}
                  \\right\rbrace
          %p In particular, the limit of a set-valued functor always exists.
          %p
            Notice the important triviality that the covariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            commutes with set-valued limits: for every set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            we have a bijection of sets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi lim
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Hom_{Set}(S, lim F)
                  \\simeq
                  \\lim Hom_{Set}(S, F(-))
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(S, F(-)) : D^{op} \to Set
          %h4#limit_of_a_functor_with_values_in_an_arbitrary_category Limit of a functor with values in an arbitrary category
          %p
            The above formula generalizes straightforwardly to a notion of limit for functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D^{op} \to C
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            an arbitrary category if we construct a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            which we will call
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi lim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \lim F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim F
            is then, if it exists, the object of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representing
            this presheaf.
          %p
            More precisely, using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y: C \to [C^{op}, Set]
            define for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D^{op} \to C
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi lim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \lim F \in [C^{op}, Set]
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi lim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi D
                            %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\hat \lim F)(c)\coloneqq Hom_{Set^{D^{op}}}(pt,Hom_C(c,F(-)))
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi lim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\hat lim F)(c) =
                  Hom(pt , Hom(c,F(-)))
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
            by an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim F
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %strong limit
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Hom(c, \lim F)
                  \\simeq
                  Hom(pt, Hom(c,F(-)))
                  \\,.
          %h4#generalization_to_weighted_limits Generalization to weighted limits
          %p
            In the above formulation, there is an evident generalization to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limits
          %p
            replace in the above the constant terminal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt : D^{op} \to Set
            with
            %em any
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W : D^{op} \to Set
            – then called the
            %em weight
            –, then the
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mi W
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\lim_W F
          %p often written
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{W,F\}
          %p is, if it exists, the object representing the presheaf
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c \mapsto
                  Hom_{[D^{op}, Set]}(W , Hom_C(c,F(-)))
                  \\,,
          %p i.e. such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mi W
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Hom(c, \lim_W F)
                  \\simeq
                  Hom(W, Hom(c,F(-)))
                  \\,
          %p
            naturally in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
          %h4#relation_to_continuous_functors Relation to continuous functors
          %p
            The very definition of limit as above asserts that the covariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(c,-) : C \to Set
            commutes with forming limits. Indeed, the definition is equivalent to saying that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functor"} continuous functor
          %h3#definition_in_terms_of_universal_cones Definition in terms of universal cones
          %p Unwrapping the above abstract definition of limits yields the following more hands-on description in terms of universal cones.
          %h4#unwrapping Unwrapping
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D^{op} \to C
            be a functor.
          %p
            Notice that for every object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
            an element
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  * \to Hom(pt, Hom(c, F(-)))
          %p is to be identified with a collection of morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c \to F(d)
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; c
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  F(d_i)
                  \&amp;&amp; \stackrel{F(f)}{\to}
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  F(d_j)
                  }
          %p
            commute. Such a collection of morphisms is called a
            %strong cone
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            If the limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim F \in C
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            exist, then it singles out a special cone given by the composite morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mrow
                          %mi lim
                          %mi F
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  *
                  \\stackrel{* \mapsto Id_{\lim F}}{\to}
                  Hom_C(\lim F, \lim F)
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                  Hom(pt, Hom(\lim F, F(-)))
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the first morphism picks the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim F
            and the second one is the defining bijection of a limit as above.
          %p The cone
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi lim
                          %mi F
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \lim F
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  F(d_i)
                  \&amp;&amp; \stackrel{F(f)}{\to}
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  F(d_j)
                  }
          %p
            is called the
            %strong universal cone
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{c \to F(d)\}_{d \in D}
            as above is bijectively related to a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \to  \lim F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi D
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  *
                  \\stackrel{\{c \to F(d)\}_{d \in D}}{\to}
                  Hom(pt, Hom(c, F(-)))
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                  Hom(c, \lim F)
                  \\,.
          %p
            By inspection one finds that, indeed, the morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi lim
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \to \lim F
            is the morphism which exhibits the factorization of the cone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{c \to F(d)\}_{d \in D}
            through the universal limit cone
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi lim
                          %mi F
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
